UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 5, Lesson 4: Presenting the story', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Presenting a story means using audible voice, planned pauses and expression matched to meaning so listeners follow events without needing the page.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMark pause and stress on: \"The ticket was gone. Sam checked his book.\" Explain why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nPause after gone (problem beat); stress ticket or book depending on the reveal plan.\n\nCHECK\nRacing every sentence at one volume makes important beats hard to hear.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPresent your narrative in 60–90 seconds from cue notes. Note one pacing change after rehearsal and one listener comment you acted on.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Presenting a story means using audible voice, planned pauses and expression matched to meaning so listeners follow events without needing the page.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Present your narrative in 60–90 seconds from cue notes. Note one pacing change after rehearsal and one listener comment you acted on. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Pause after gone (problem beat); stress ticket or book depending on the reveal plan. Reasoning: I used present by attending to story rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Racing every sentence at one volume makes important beats hard to hear. Correction: Pause after gone (problem beat); stress ticket or book depending on the reveal plan. Why: the correction restores accurate use of present.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Racing every sentence at one volume makes important beats hard to hear. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating present on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with present, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Pause after gone (problem beat); stress ticket or book depending on the reveal plan. This matches the lesson skill for present.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Pause after gone (problem beat); stress ticket or book depending on the reveal plan.","Racing every sentence at one volume makes important beats hard to hear."],"question":"Mark pause and stress on: \"The ticket was gone. Sam checked his book.\" Explain why. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Racing every sentence at one volume makes important beats hard to hear. T